Jym wrote:

> Are these shopkeepers really stupid or what?

Well pretty much so, but that's by design. Robbing
shops in NetHack is a _feature_, and that feature
requires shopkeepers to behave pretty stupidly.

The rest of that story, though, is that arguments
against credit cloning that include "no shopkeeper
would be that stupid" as a reason for turning it off
are ignoring the datum that in NetHack, shopkeepers
can be and _are_ "that stupid", so such arguments
should not prevail. They violate the "NetHack is not
Real Life" precept.

Arguments that credit cloning throws the game "far
out of balance" therefore need to be pretty
carefully crafted to have any hope of being
convincing.

All that is changed by credit cloning is _how fast_
the PC can bankrupt a shopkeeper and steal all that
shopkeeper's goods. The possibility is always there
whether pets can steal gold off of the floor of the
shop or not, so the only balance changed is the
"food versus time to clean out a shop versus
danger from arriving monsters" one, not the "can you
do this shop robbing at all" one.

For a protection racketeer, who lets Phideaux kill
all the arriving monsters except the first couple to
put alignment at max for food prayer purposes, that
former balance is a non-issue anyway, at least for
shops at reasonable Dlvls. The arriving monsters
will remain relative wimps, the ever-more-powerful
pet will kill them more and more easily, and prayers
for food will always succeed unless RL luck is
abysmal.

Credit cloning, in the end, then, just makes a
really boring patience game process of robbing shops
go _faster_ in wall clock time, with little other
game effect.

Well, except for the non-game effect that it
infuriates people who think patience gaming
shouldn't be an allowable way of winning at NetHack
_at all_, and so detest credit cloning, pudding
farming, polypiling, stash management, altar
squatting, extinctionism, and all the other patience
game techniques pretty much as an indivisible kit.

I don't sympathize with people who want NetHack to
be all about speed play and twitch reflexes, in the
least. I've probably made that abundantly obvious.

If I wanted to play Doom, I'd play Doom, not waste
my life trying to make NetHack play be conceptually
indistinguishable from Doom play, with the
understood purpose of forcing everyone else to play
NetHack like Doom just because that's the way _I_
play NetHack, and so such a playing style is
obviously The Only Way to be Fair to Everyone and is
thus by definition The One True Way.

